  await prisma.fullExamQuestion.createMany({
    data: [
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 1, questionText: 'A patient with ARDS has the following ventilator settings: VT 350 mL (6 mL/kg PBW), RR 28, FiO2 0.80, PEEP 16 cmH2O. Plateau pressure is 28 cmH2O and PaO2 is 62 mmHg. What adjustment is most appropriate?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Increase tidal volume to 8 mL/kg PBW', B: 'Decrease PEEP to 10 cmH2O', C: 'Increase FiO2 to 1.0 and consider prone positioning', D: 'Decrease respiratory rate to 18' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'With plateau pressure of 28 (below 30 cmH2O limit), PEEP already at 16, and persistent hypoxemia, increasing FiO2 and initiating prone positioning (if P/F < 150) is appropriate before considering higher PEEP.', explanationWrong: 'Increasing VT above 6 mL/kg risks VILI. Decreasing PEEP would worsen oxygenation. Decreasing RR would reduce minute ventilation and worsen any hypercapnia.', topic: 'Advanced Mechanical Ventilation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 2, questionText: 'A critically ill patient has a cardiac index of 1.9 L/min/m2, SVR of 2200 dynes·sec·cm⁻⁵, PAWP of 24 mmHg, and CVP of 14 mmHg. The appropriate treatment is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Volume resuscitation with crystalloid', B: 'Vasopressor therapy with norepinephrine', C: 'Inotropic support with dobutamine and possible vasodilator', D: 'Vasodilator therapy with nitroglycerin' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'This hemodynamic profile (low CI, high SVR, high PAWP, elevated CVP) represents cardiogenic shock. Treatment includes inotropic support (dobutamine) to improve contractility and afterload reduction.', explanationWrong: 'Volume resuscitation would worsen pulmonary edema (PAWP already 24). Vasopressors would increase afterload on a failing heart. Vasodilators alone would drop BP without improving output.', topic: 'Hemodynamic Monitoring' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 3, questionText: 'A patient on a ventilator has pH 7.48, PaCO2 28 mmHg, HCO3- 21 mEq/L. The patient is anxious and tachypneic. The most appropriate intervention is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Address the cause of anxiety/pain and adjust ventilator rate if controlled', B: 'Increase tidal volume', C: 'Add dead space to the circuit', D: 'Administer IV sodium bicarbonate' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'This is acute respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation. Addressing the underlying cause (anxiety, pain, hypoxia) is the primary intervention. If on controlled ventilation, reducing the set rate may help.', explanationWrong: 'Increasing VT would worsen alkalosis. Adding dead space is a temporizing measure. Bicarbonate would worsen alkalosis. Treating the cause of hyperventilation is most appropriate.', topic: 'Advanced ABG Interpretation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 4, questionText: 'The Berlin definition of ARDS requires respiratory failure NOT fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload. Which test best helps make this distinction?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Complete blood count', B: 'Echocardiography', C: 'Sputum culture', D: 'Liver function tests' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Echocardiography assesses left ventricular function and helps differentiate ARDS (normal LV function) from cardiogenic pulmonary edema (impaired LV function, elevated filling pressures).', explanationWrong: 'CBC, sputum culture, and LFTs do not differentiate ARDS from cardiogenic edema. Echocardiography directly evaluates cardiac function and filling pressures.', topic: 'ARDS Diagnosis and Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 5, questionText: 'The "hour-1 bundle" in sepsis management includes all of the following EXCEPT:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Measure lactate level', B: 'Obtain blood cultures before antibiotics', C: 'Administer broad-spectrum antibiotics', D: 'Obtain pulmonary function testing' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Pulmonary function testing is not part of the hour-1 bundle and has no role in acute sepsis management. The bundle includes: measure lactate, obtain blood cultures, administer antibiotics, begin fluid resuscitation for hypotension or lactate ≥ 4, and apply vasopressors if needed.', explanationWrong: 'Lactate measurement, blood cultures, and broad-spectrum antibiotics are all part of the hour-1 bundle. PFTs are elective outpatient tests with no role in acute sepsis management.', topic: 'Sepsis and Septic Shock' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 6, questionText: 'The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the RIGHT with:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Fetal hemoglobin', B: 'Increased pH, decreased temperature, decreased 2,3-DPG', C: 'Carbon monoxide exposure', D: 'Decreased pH, increased temperature, increased 2,3-DPG, and increased PaCO2' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'A rightward shift occurs with decreased pH (acidosis), increased temperature, increased 2,3-DPG, and increased PaCO2 - conditions that favor oxygen unloading to tissues. This is the Bohr effect.', explanationWrong: 'Increased pH causes left shift. CO and fetal hemoglobin cause left shift. Right shift occurs with acidosis, fever, hypercarbia, and elevated 2,3-DPG.', topic: 'Acute Respiratory Failure' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 7, questionText: 'During an SBT, which of the following warrants immediate termination of the trial?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Respiratory rate of 22 breaths/min', B: 'New onset of cardiac arrhythmia or hemodynamic instability', C: 'Mild anxiety', D: 'Slight increase in work of breathing' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'SBT failure criteria requiring immediate termination include: new arrhythmia, hemodynamic instability, SpO2 < 88-90%, RR > 35, significant distress, or diaphoresis.', explanationWrong: 'RR of 22 is normal. Mild anxiety may resolve with reassurance. Slight increased WOB is expected. New arrhythmias and hemodynamic instability require immediate termination.', topic: 'Weaning Protocols and SBTs' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 8, questionText: 'In a patient with acute hypercapnic respiratory failure from COPD, BiPAP is preferred over CPAP because:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'CPAP is contraindicated in COPD', B: 'BiPAP is less expensive', C: 'BiPAP provides both inspiratory pressure support (to augment ventilation) and expiratory pressure (to maintain airway patency)', D: 'CPAP causes more air trapping' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'BiPAP provides IPAP for ventilatory support (reducing WOB and assisting CO2 removal) and EPAP for preventing airway collapse and offsetting auto-PEEP. CPAP provides only one level of pressure.', explanationWrong: 'CPAP is not contraindicated in COPD but does not augment ventilation. BiPAP is generally more expensive. CPAP does not specifically cause more air trapping. BiPAPs dual pressure is the key advantage.', topic: 'NIV in Critical Care' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 9, questionText: 'Which team member typically leads the coordination of turning a patient prone?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'The pharmacist', B: 'The respiratory therapist managing the airway', C: 'The dietitian', D: 'The social worker' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'The respiratory therapist or physician managing the airway typically leads prone positioning to ensure ETT security during the turn. The RT manages the airway, ventilator circuit, and confirms tube position.', explanationWrong: 'Pharmacists, dietitians, and social workers have important ICU roles but do not lead the prone positioning procedure. Airway management expertise is critical during the turn.', topic: 'Prone Positioning' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 10, questionText: 'Which clinical scenario is the strongest indication for neuromuscular blockade in the ICU?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Early severe ARDS with P/F < 150 despite deep sedation and ventilator dyssynchrony', B: 'Mild asthma exacerbation', C: 'Stable patient awaiting extubation', D: 'Patient with chronic pain' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'NMB is indicated for severe ARDS (P/F < 150) with refractory dyssynchrony despite adequate sedation, particularly in the first 48 hours when it may improve outcomes per the ACURASYS trial.', explanationWrong: 'Mild asthma does not require paralysis. Patients awaiting extubation need spontaneous breathing assessment. Chronic pain is managed with analgesics, not paralytics.', topic: 'Neuromuscular Blocking Agents' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 11, questionText: 'Propofol infusion syndrome (PRIS) is characterized by:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia, cardiac failure, and lipemia', B: 'Mild nausea and vomiting', C: 'Skin rash', D: 'Hypertension and tachycardia only' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'PRIS is a rare but potentially fatal complication of prolonged high-dose propofol infusion (>5 mg/kg/h for >48 hours), presenting with severe metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia, renal failure, and cardiovascular collapse.', explanationWrong: 'PRIS is far more serious than hypertension, nausea, or rash. It involves multi-organ failure and is often fatal if not recognized early. Propofol should be monitored for dose and duration.', topic: 'Sedation and Pain Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 12, questionText: 'The VAP prevention bundle element that involves keeping patients in a semi-recumbent position targets:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Improved cardiac output', B: 'Reduced intracranial pressure', C: 'Reduction of gastric content aspiration into the lower airway', D: 'Improved patient comfort for sleep' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'Semi-recumbent positioning (HOB 30-45 degrees) reduces aspiration of gastric contents and oropharyngeal secretions past the ETT cuff into the lower airway, a primary mechanism of VAP development.', explanationWrong: 'While HOB elevation may incidentally affect ICP and cardiac function, the specific VAP bundle rationale is reduction of aspiration. Comfort is secondary.', topic: 'VAP Prevention' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 13, questionText: 'Bridge to transplant is an indication for ECMO in which patient population?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Patients with end-stage lung or heart disease awaiting organ transplant', B: 'All ICU patients with pneumonia', C: 'Post-surgical patients with mild hypoxemia', D: 'Patients with stable COPD' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'ECMO serves as a bridge to transplant for patients with end-stage cardiopulmonary disease who would die before a donor organ becomes available. It maintains organ perfusion and function while waiting.', explanationWrong: 'Not all pneumonia patients need ECMO. Mild hypoxemia does not warrant ECMO. Stable COPD is managed medically. Bridge-to-transplant is for end-stage disease in transplant candidates.', topic: 'ECMO' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 14, questionText: 'Traumatic aortic injury following rapid deceleration most commonly occurs at:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'The ascending aorta', B: 'The abdominal aorta', C: 'The aortic isthmus (just distal to the left subclavian artery)', D: 'The aortic valve' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'The aortic isthmus is the most common site of traumatic aortic injury because it is a transition point between the mobile aortic arch and the fixed descending thoracic aorta, creating shear forces during deceleration.', explanationWrong: 'The ascending aorta and abdominal aorta are less common sites. The aortic valve is injured by different mechanisms. The isthmus is the classic location for deceleration injuries.', topic: 'Chest Trauma' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 15, questionText: 'In burn patients, the Lund and Browder chart is preferred over the Rule of Nines because:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'It is simpler to use', B: 'It accounts for age-related differences in body proportions', C: 'It requires no training', D: 'It only measures second-degree burns' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'The Lund and Browder chart provides more accurate TBSA estimation by accounting for age-related variations in body surface area proportions, particularly important in children whose head represents a larger percentage.', explanationWrong: 'The Lund and Browder chart is actually more complex. It requires training to use. It assesses all burn depths. Its advantage is age-adjusted accuracy.', topic: 'Burns and Inhalation Injury' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 16, questionText: 'Non-invasive ventilation (NIV) in acute COPD exacerbation is recommended when pH falls below:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: '7.45', B: '7.40', C: '7.30', D: '7.35' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'NIV is recommended for COPD exacerbation with respiratory acidosis (pH < 7.35) and PaCO2 > 45 mmHg despite initial medical therapy. Stronger evidence supports NIV when pH is 7.25-7.35.', explanationWrong: '7.45 and 7.40 are normal pH values. pH < 7.30 may indicate need for intubation if NIV fails. The threshold to initiate NIV is pH < 7.35 with hypercapnia.', topic: 'Acute COPD Exacerbation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 17, questionText: 'Severe, life-threatening asthma is indicated by which finding?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Mild wheezing on exertion only', B: 'Peak flow 60-80% of predicted', C: 'Silent chest with absent breath sounds', D: 'SpO2 of 96% on room air' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'A silent chest in asthma indicates critically severe bronchospasm with virtually no airflow. The absence of wheezing is ominous and indicates near-complete airway obstruction requiring emergent intervention.', explanationWrong: 'Mild wheezing and PEF 60-80% indicate moderate severity. SpO2 96% is normal. A silent chest is the most dangerous finding, indicating minimal air movement.', topic: 'Status Asthmaticus' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 18, questionText: 'Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) following PE is best treated with:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Long-term antibiotics', B: 'Continued anticoagulation alone', C: 'Bronchodilators', D: 'Pulmonary thromboendarterectomy (PTE)' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'PTE surgery is the definitive treatment for CTEPH, offering potential cure by surgically removing organized thrombus from the pulmonary arteries. Medical therapy with riociguat is an alternative for inoperable patients.', explanationWrong: 'Antibiotics are irrelevant. Anticoagulation alone does not resolve organized thrombus. Bronchodilators do not address the vascular pathology. Surgical PTE is the treatment of choice.', topic: 'Pulmonary Embolism' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 19, questionText: 'A tension pneumothorax should be treated with immediate:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Needle decompression at the 2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line (or 4th-5th ICS, anterior axillary line)', B: 'Chest CT scan', C: 'Observation and repeat chest X-ray', D: 'Bronchoscopy' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis requiring emergent needle decompression (14-gauge needle at the 2nd ICS MCL or 4th-5th ICS AAL) followed by chest tube insertion. Imaging should not delay treatment.', explanationWrong: 'CT scan delays treatment of a life-threatening emergency. Observation risks cardiopulmonary arrest. Bronchoscopy is not the treatment. Immediate decompression is mandatory.', topic: 'Pleural Diseases' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 20, questionText: 'The Leapfrog Group ICU staffing standard recommends:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'One nurse per 10 ICU patients', B: 'Intensivist physicians present or available during daytime hours with a goal of 24/7 coverage', C: 'No physician coverage on weekends', D: 'Respiratory therapists only during business hours' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'The Leapfrog Group recommends that ICU patients be managed or co-managed by board-certified intensivists who are present during the day and available at all times, with 24/7 coverage as the goal.', explanationWrong: '1:10 nursing ratio is inadequate for ICU (1:1-2 is standard). No weekend coverage compromises safety. RT should provide continuous coverage. Intensivist presence improves outcomes.', topic: 'ICU Quality Metrics' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 21, questionText: 'Pressure-regulated volume control (PRVC) ventilation provides:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Guaranteed tidal volume with volume-control waveforms', B: 'No pressure or volume targets', C: 'Only pressure-targeted breaths with no volume guarantee', D: 'Adaptive pressure-controlled breaths that target a set tidal volume', }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'PRVC is a dual-control mode that delivers pressure-controlled breaths but automatically adjusts the inspiratory pressure breath-by-breath to achieve a target tidal volume, combining benefits of both modes.', explanationWrong: 'PRVC is not simple volume control (different waveform) nor pure pressure control (has VT target). It provides a hybrid approach with adaptive pressure targeting a set volume.', topic: 'Advanced Mechanical Ventilation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 22, questionText: 'Passive leg raise (PLR) test is used to predict:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Deep vein thrombosis', B: 'Peripheral neuropathy', C: 'Hip fracture', D: 'Fluid responsiveness without administering fluid' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'PLR predicts fluid responsiveness by transiently shifting approximately 300 mL of blood from the lower extremities to the central circulation, simulating a fluid bolus. A cardiac output increase of ≥10% predicts fluid responsiveness.', explanationWrong: 'PLR is not for DVT diagnosis, neuropathy assessment, or fracture detection. It is a reversible hemodynamic test that predicts fluid responsiveness without actual fluid administration.', topic: 'Hemodynamic Monitoring' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 23, questionText: 'A patient with salicylate toxicity typically presents with which acid-base pattern?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Simple metabolic alkalosis', B: 'Mixed respiratory alkalosis and anion gap metabolic acidosis', C: 'Simple respiratory acidosis', D: 'Normal acid-base status' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Salicylate toxicity causes direct stimulation of the respiratory center (respiratory alkalosis) and uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation (anion gap metabolic acidosis), creating a characteristic mixed disorder.', explanationWrong: 'Simple alkalosis or acidosis alone does not explain the dual mechanism. Normal status is incompatible with significant toxicity. The mixed pattern is pathognomonic.', topic: 'Advanced ABG Interpretation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 24, questionText: 'The ARDS severity classification "moderate" corresponds to a PaO2/FiO2 ratio of:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: '201-300 mmHg', B: '> 300 mmHg', C: '101-200 mmHg', D: '≤ 100 mmHg' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'The Berlin definition classifies moderate ARDS as PaO2/FiO2 101-200 mmHg with PEEP ≥ 5 cmH2O. Mild is 201-300, and severe is ≤ 100 mmHg.', explanationWrong: '201-300 is mild. > 300 is normal. ≤ 100 is severe. Moderate ARDS is the middle category at 101-200 mmHg.', topic: 'ARDS Diagnosis and Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 25, questionText: 'Sepsis-induced tissue hypoperfusion is indicated by:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Normal vital signs', B: 'Serum lactate ≥ 4 mmol/L or hypotension persisting after initial fluid bolus', C: 'Elevated serum albumin', D: 'Low white blood cell count only' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Lactate ≥ 4 mmol/L indicates significant tissue hypoperfusion even without hypotension. Persistent hypotension after 30 mL/kg crystalloid bolus also indicates tissue hypoperfusion requiring aggressive resuscitation.', explanationWrong: 'Normal vitals suggest adequate perfusion. Elevated albumin is not an indicator. Low WBC alone does not define hypoperfusion. Lactate and refractory hypotension are the key markers.', topic: 'Sepsis and Septic Shock' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 26, questionText: 'The P50 value on the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve represents:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'The PaO2 at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated', B: 'The maximum oxygen content of blood', C: 'The minimum PaO2 for survival', D: 'The pressure at which all hemoglobin is saturated' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'P50 is the partial pressure of oxygen at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated. Normal P50 is approximately 26.6 mmHg. An increased P50 indicates right shift (easier O2 unloading); decreased P50 indicates left shift.', explanationWrong: 'Maximum O2 content depends on hemoglobin concentration. Minimum PaO2 for survival varies. Complete saturation occurs around PaO2 of 100+. P50 specifically refers to 50% saturation.', topic: 'Acute Respiratory Failure' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 27, questionText: 'The maximum duration for a spontaneous breathing trial recommended by current guidelines is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: '30-120 minutes', B: '15 minutes', C: '2 hours', D: '5 minutes' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Current guidelines recommend SBT duration of 30-120 minutes. Evidence supports 30-minute trials as non-inferior to 120-minute trials. Most failures manifest within the first 30 minutes.', explanationWrong: '5 and 15 minutes are too short to adequately assess. While 2 hours is within the range, the complete recommended range is 30-120 minutes.', topic: 'Weaning Protocols and SBTs' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 28, questionText: 'Heated humidification is essential during high-flow nasal cannula therapy because:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'It sterilizes the delivered gas', B: 'It reduces the cost of treatment', C: 'It prevents drying of the nasal mucosa and improves mucociliary clearance at high flow rates', D: 'It increases the FiO2 delivered' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'High-flow rates without heated humidification would severely dry the nasal and airway mucosa, causing discomfort, mucosal injury, and impaired mucociliary function. Heated humidification to 37°C and 100% relative humidity is essential.', explanationWrong: 'Humidification does not sterilize gas, reduce costs, or change FiO2. Its purpose is mucosal protection and patient comfort at high flow rates.', topic: 'NIV in Critical Care' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 29, questionText: 'Contraindications to prone positioning include all of the following EXCEPT:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Presence of bilateral chest tubes', B: 'Open abdominal wound', C: 'Hemodynamic instability requiring escalating vasopressors', D: 'Unstable spinal injury' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Bilateral chest tubes are NOT a contraindication to prone positioning. Patients with chest tubes can be safely proned with proper tube management. Unstable spinal injury, open abdomen, and hemodynamic instability requiring escalating vasopressors are true contraindications.', explanationWrong: 'Unstable spine, open abdomen, and hemodynamic instability are recognized contraindications. Chest tubes are managed during proning and are not a contraindication.', topic: 'Prone Positioning' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 30, questionText: 'Neostigmine, used to reverse nondepolarizing NMBAs, works by:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Direct stimulation of skeletal muscle', B: 'Binding to nicotinic receptors', C: 'Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase to increase acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction', D: 'Blocking calcium channels' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'Neostigmine is an anticholinesterase that inhibits the breakdown of acetylcholine at the NMJ, increasing ACh concentration to competitively displace nondepolarizing NMBAs from nicotinic receptors.', explanationWrong: 'Neostigmine does not directly stimulate muscle or bind nicotinic receptors. It does not block calcium channels. It works indirectly by preserving acetylcholine at the junction.', topic: 'Neuromuscular Blocking Agents' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 31, questionText: 'The Confusion Assessment Method for the ICU (CAM-ICU) assesses which four features?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Pain, sedation, mobility, and nutrition', B: 'Acute onset/fluctuating course, inattention, disorganized thinking, and altered level of consciousness', C: 'Heart rate, blood pressure, respiratory rate, and temperature', D: 'Anxiety, depression, insomnia, and appetite' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'CAM-ICU evaluates: (1) acute onset or fluctuating course, (2) inattention, (3) altered level of consciousness, and (4) disorganized thinking. Features 1+2 plus either 3 or 4 = positive for delirium.', explanationWrong: 'Pain/sedation are separate assessments. Vital signs do not detect delirium. Psychiatric symptoms are different from delirium features. The four CAM-ICU features specifically detect delirium.', topic: 'Sedation and Pain Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 32, questionText: 'Peptic ulcer disease prophylaxis in mechanically ventilated ICU patients is typically achieved with:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Antacids only', B: 'Dietary modifications', C: 'Surgical fundoplication', D: 'Proton pump inhibitors or H2 receptor antagonists' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Stress ulcer prophylaxis in ventilated ICU patients uses PPIs (pantoprazole) or H2RAs (famotidine). The SUP-ICU trial and PEPTIC trial compared these classes, with clinical practice favoring PPIs.', explanationWrong: 'Antacids alone are insufficient. Dietary modifications are impractical in ventilated patients. Surgery is not prophylactic. Pharmacologic acid suppression is the standard approach.', topic: 'VAP Prevention' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 33, questionText: 'The main advantage of a dual-lumen single cannula (Avalon Elite) for VV ECMO is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Lower cost', B: 'Easier insertion', C: 'Single-site cannulation (right internal jugular) preserving patient mobility', D: 'Higher flow rates than standard two-site cannulation' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'The bicaval dual-lumen cannula is placed via the right IJ, with drainage from SVC/IVC and return directed toward the tricuspid valve. Single-site insertion allows patient ambulation during ECMO, important for bridge-to-transplant patients.', explanationWrong: 'Dual-lumen cannulae are more expensive and require fluoroscopic/echo guidance for placement. Flow rates may be lower than two-site configurations. Mobility is the key advantage.', topic: 'ECMO' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 34, questionText: 'Massive transfusion protocol activation is generally triggered at:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Hemoglobin < 10 g/dL', B: 'Any minor laceration', C: 'Mild anemia', D: 'Anticipated need for ≥ 10 units pRBCs within 24 hours or hemorrhagic shock with ongoing bleeding' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Massive transfusion protocol is activated when ≥ 10 units pRBCs are anticipated in 24 hours, ≥ 4 units in 1 hour, or the patient has hemorrhagic shock with active bleeding. This triggers a balanced 1:1:1 resuscitation protocol.', explanationWrong: 'Hgb < 10 alone does not trigger MTP. Minor lacerations do not require it. Mild anemia is not an indication. MTP is for life-threatening hemorrhage requiring massive blood product resuscitation.', topic: 'Chest Trauma' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 35, questionText: 'Fluid creep in burn resuscitation refers to:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Excessive fluid administration beyond Parkland formula calculations', B: 'Normal fluid maintenance', C: 'Electrolyte imbalances only', D: 'Inadequate fluid administration' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Fluid creep refers to the tendency to administer excessive fluid volumes beyond formula calculations in burn patients, leading to complications such as abdominal compartment syndrome, pulmonary edema, and extremity compartment syndrome.', explanationWrong: 'Fluid creep is over-resuscitation, not under-resuscitation. It is not normal maintenance or just electrolyte issues. It is a recognized complication of burn resuscitation requiring close urine output monitoring.', topic: 'Burns and Inhalation Injury' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 36, questionText: 'Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency predisposes patients to:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Asthma only', B: 'Early-onset emphysema, particularly in the lower lobes', C: 'Pulmonary hypertension only', D: 'Upper lobe emphysema' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'AAT deficiency allows unopposed neutrophil elastase activity, destroying alveolar walls and causing panacinar emphysema predominantly in the lung bases. This contrasts with smoking-related centrilobular emphysema in upper lobes.', explanationWrong: 'AAT deficiency causes emphysema, not just asthma or PH. The distribution is characteristically lower lobe (basilar), not upper lobe as seen in smoking-related disease.', topic: 'Acute COPD Exacerbation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 37, questionText: 'During mechanical ventilation for status asthmaticus, auto-PEEP is measured by performing:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'A maximum inspiratory pressure maneuver', B: 'An arterial blood gas', C: 'A chest X-ray', D: 'An end-expiratory hold maneuver' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Auto-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP) is measured by performing an end-expiratory pause/hold, which equilibrates airway pressure with alveolar pressure. The difference between total PEEP and set PEEP is auto-PEEP.', explanationWrong: 'MIP measures inspiratory muscle strength. ABG shows gas exchange but not auto-PEEP. CXR may show hyperinflation but cannot quantify auto-PEEP. End-expiratory hold directly measures it.', topic: 'Status Asthmaticus' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 38, questionText: 'The most common ECG finding in pulmonary embolism is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'ST elevation in all leads', B: 'S1Q3T3 pattern', C: 'Complete heart block', D: 'Sinus tachycardia' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Sinus tachycardia is the most common ECG finding in PE, present in approximately 40-50% of cases. The classic S1Q3T3 pattern is present in only 10-20% of PE cases.', explanationWrong: 'S1Q3T3 is classic but uncommon. Complete heart block is rare. Diffuse ST elevation suggests pericarditis. Sinus tachycardia is by far the most frequent finding.', topic: 'Pulmonary Embolism' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 39, questionText: 'Pleurodesis is performed to:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Diagnose pleural effusion', B: 'Prevent recurrent pleural effusion or pneumothorax by creating adhesion between visceral and parietal pleura', C: 'Increase pleural fluid production', D: 'Treat pneumonia' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Pleurodesis involves instilling a sclerosing agent (talc, doxycycline) into the pleural space to create inflammation and adhesion between pleural layers, obliterating the space and preventing fluid or air reaccumulation.', explanationWrong: 'Diagnosis uses thoracentesis. Pleurodesis prevents (not increases) fluid accumulation. Pneumonia is treated with antibiotics. Pleurodesis is a definitive treatment for recurrent effusions or pneumothorax.', topic: 'Pleural Diseases' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 40, questionText: 'ICU readmission within 48 hours is a quality metric that reflects:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Efficient bed utilization', B: 'Excellent discharge planning', C: 'Potentially premature ICU discharge or inadequate step-down care', D: 'Normal patient flow' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'ICU readmission within 48 hours (bounce-back rate) suggests patients may have been discharged prematurely or that the receiving unit lacked adequate monitoring/care capability. Target rates are typically < 5%.', explanationWrong: 'Early readmission indicates a problem, not efficiency or good planning. It is not normal flow. It is a specific quality indicator tracked to improve discharge decision-making.', topic: 'ICU Quality Metrics' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 41, questionText: 'Proportional assist ventilation (PAV+) differs from pressure support ventilation in that PAV+ provides:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'A fixed level of support regardless of patient effort', B: 'Variable support proportional to the patient instantaneous effort', C: 'No patient triggering', D: 'Only volume-targeted breaths' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'PAV+ measures respiratory system mechanics and delivers pressure proportional to instantaneous patient effort (flow and volume demand). As effort increases, support increases; as effort decreases, support decreases.', explanationWrong: 'Fixed support describes PSV. PAV+ requires patient triggering. It is not volume-targeted. Its unique feature is effort-proportional variable assistance.', topic: 'Advanced Mechanical Ventilation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 42, questionText: 'The Fick principle for cardiac output calculation requires measurement of:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Oxygen consumption, arterial O2 content, and mixed venous O2 content', B: 'Heart rate and blood pressure only', C: 'Respiratory rate and tidal volume', D: 'Urine output and serum creatinine' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Fick equation: CO = VO2 / (CaO2 - CvO2). It requires oxygen consumption (VO2), arterial oxygen content (CaO2), and mixed venous oxygen content (CvO2 from PA catheter).', explanationWrong: 'HR and BP are hemodynamic parameters but insufficient for Fick calculation. RR and VT relate to ventilation. Urine output relates to renal function. Fick specifically uses oxygen variables.', topic: 'Hemodynamic Monitoring' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 43, questionText: 'A patient has pH 7.38, PaCO2 56 mmHg, HCO3- 32 mEq/L. This pattern represents:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Compensated (chronic) respiratory acidosis', B: 'Normal acid-base status', C: 'Metabolic alkalosis', D: 'Acute respiratory acidosis' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Near-normal pH with elevated PaCO2 and elevated HCO3- indicates chronic respiratory acidosis with complete renal compensation. The kidneys have retained bicarbonate to normalize pH (expected HCO3- rise ~3.5 mEq/L per 10 mmHg PaCO2 increase chronically).', explanationWrong: 'Acute respiratory acidosis would have lower pH (minimal compensation). pH 7.38 is not entirely normal given the elevated PaCO2. Primary metabolic alkalosis would have higher pH.', topic: 'Advanced ABG Interpretation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 44, questionText: 'Prone positioning in ARDS improves survival primarily through:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Reducing ICU costs', B: 'Improving patient comfort', C: 'Reducing ventilator-induced lung injury by promoting more homogeneous ventilation', D: 'Increasing drug absorption' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'The mortality benefit of prone positioning is attributed to reduced VILI from more homogeneous ventilation distribution, decreased cyclic recruitment/derecruitment, and improved regional V/Q matching, not just improved oxygenation.', explanationWrong: 'Cost reduction and comfort are not the primary mechanisms. Drug absorption is not affected. The survival benefit is from reduced lung injury through improved ventilation homogeneity.', topic: 'ARDS Diagnosis and Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 45, questionText: 'The Surviving Sepsis Campaign recommends against using which type of fluid for initial resuscitation?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Hydroxyethyl starch (HES) solutions', B: 'Lactated Ringer solution', C: 'Normal saline', D: 'Plasmalyte' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'HES solutions are strongly recommended against in sepsis resuscitation due to increased risk of acute kidney injury and need for renal replacement therapy, as demonstrated in the VISEP and 6S trials.', explanationWrong: 'LR, NS, and Plasmalyte are acceptable crystalloid choices. Balanced crystalloids (LR, Plasmalyte) may be preferred over NS. Only HES is specifically contraindicated.', topic: 'Sepsis and Septic Shock' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 46, questionText: 'Compliance of the respiratory system is calculated as:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Peak pressure divided by tidal volume', B: 'Tidal volume divided by resistance', C: 'Tidal volume divided by (plateau pressure minus PEEP)', D: 'Respiratory rate multiplied by tidal volume' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'Static compliance = VT / (Pplat - PEEP). Normal is 60-100 mL/cmH2O. Decreased compliance (< 30) is seen in ARDS, pneumothorax, pulmonary fibrosis, and abdominal distension.', explanationWrong: 'PIP/VT involves resistive components. VT/resistance is not compliance. RR x VT is minute ventilation. Compliance specifically uses VT divided by driving pressure.', topic: 'Acute Respiratory Failure' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 47, questionText: 'Tracheostomy in mechanically ventilated patients is typically considered after:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: '24 hours of intubation', B: '7-14 days if prolonged ventilation is anticipated', C: '30 days of intubation', D: 'Only after failed extubation' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Early tracheostomy (7-14 days) is considered when prolonged mechanical ventilation is anticipated. Benefits include reduced sedation requirements, improved patient comfort, easier nursing care, and potentially faster weaning.', explanationWrong: '24 hours is too early. 30 days risks tracheal injury from prolonged translaryngeal intubation. Failed extubation alone is not the only indication. 7-14 days with expected prolonged ventilation is the typical timeframe.', topic: 'Weaning Protocols and SBTs' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 48, questionText: 'Adaptive support ventilation (ASV) automatically adjusts:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'FiO2 based on SpO2', B: 'PEEP based on compliance', C: 'Nothing - all settings are manual', D: 'Respiratory rate, tidal volume, and inspiratory time to minimize work of breathing based on patient mechanics' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'ASV automatically adjusts respiratory rate, tidal volume, and inspiratory time based on measured respiratory mechanics to deliver target minute ventilation with minimal work of breathing, using the Otis-Mead equation of optimal breathing frequency.', explanationWrong: 'ASV does not adjust FiO2 or PEEP (those remain manual). It is not fully manual. Its unique feature is automatic optimization of breathing pattern based on respiratory mechanics.', topic: 'NIV in Critical Care' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 49, questionText: 'Eye care during prone positioning is essential to prevent:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Hearing loss', B: 'Dental injury', C: 'Corneal abrasions and ischemic optic neuropathy', D: 'Urinary retention' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'Prone positioning increases intraocular pressure and risks corneal abrasion from direct pressure. Eyes should be closed with protective covers, and periorbital edema should be monitored. Ischemic optic neuropathy can cause permanent vision loss.', explanationWrong: 'Hearing loss, dental injury, and urinary retention are not specific eye-care concerns. Corneal abrasions and optic neuropathy are the specific ocular complications of prone positioning.', topic: 'Prone Positioning' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 50, questionText: 'Rocuronium and vecuronium belong to which class of neuromuscular blocking agents?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Depolarizing agents', B: 'Cholinesterase inhibitors', C: 'Benzodiazepines', D: 'Aminosteroid nondepolarizing agents' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Rocuronium and vecuronium are aminosteroid nondepolarizing NMBAs. They competitively block acetylcholine at the nicotinic receptor without causing depolarization. Cisatracurium and atracurium are benzylisoquinoline nondepolarizing agents.', explanationWrong: 'Depolarizing agents include only succinylcholine. Cholinesterase inhibitors reverse NMBAs. Benzodiazepines are sedatives. Rocuronium and vecuronium are aminosteroid nondepolarizing agents.', topic: 'Neuromuscular Blocking Agents' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 51, questionText: 'Ketamine as a sedative agent in the ICU is notable because it:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Maintains hemodynamic stability and preserves respiratory drive', B: 'Causes significant respiratory depression', C: 'Requires mechanical ventilation in all cases', D: 'Has no analgesic properties' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Ketamine maintains blood pressure through sympathomimetic effects and preserves respiratory drive, making it useful for sedation in hemodynamically unstable patients or those at risk for hypotension from other sedatives.', explanationWrong: 'Ketamine is unique in preserving hemodynamics and respiratory drive. It does not require ventilation for procedural sedation. It has potent analgesic properties (NMDA antagonism).', topic: 'Sedation and Pain Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 52, questionText: 'Continuous lateral rotation therapy (CLRT) beds in the ICU help prevent:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Deep vein thrombosis only', B: 'Ventilator-associated pneumonia through enhanced secretion mobilization', C: 'Urinary tract infections', D: 'Medication errors' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'CLRT beds continuously rotate the patient side to side, promoting pulmonary secretion drainage and preventing atelectasis. Some studies show reduced VAP rates with continuous rotation therapy.', explanationWrong: 'While CLRT may help prevent DVT, its primary respiratory benefit is VAP reduction through secretion mobilization. It does not prevent UTIs or medication errors.', topic: 'VAP Prevention' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 53, questionText: 'ECMO candidacy generally requires that the underlying condition be:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Irreversible', B: 'Present for more than 30 days', C: 'Mild and self-limiting', D: 'Potentially reversible or the patient is a transplant candidate' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'ECMO is indicated when the underlying condition is reversible (bridge to recovery), the patient is a transplant candidate (bridge to transplant), or a destination therapy decision is pending (bridge to decision).', explanationWrong: 'Irreversible disease without transplant candidacy is generally a contraindication. Duration is not the primary criterion. Mild conditions do not require ECMO. Reversibility or transplant candidacy is essential.', topic: 'ECMO' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 54, questionText: 'Sucking chest wound (open pneumothorax) initial treatment involves:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Immediate intubation', B: 'Leaving the wound completely open', C: 'Applying a fully occlusive dressing', D: 'Applying a three-sided occlusive dressing' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'A three-sided occlusive dressing creates a flutter-valve effect: the sealed sides prevent air entry during inspiration, while the open side allows air escape during expiration, preventing tension pneumothorax development.', explanationWrong: 'An open wound continues to suck air. A fully occlusive dressing can convert to tension pneumothorax. Intubation alone does not address the chest wall defect. Three-sided dressing is the field treatment.', topic: 'Chest Trauma' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 55, questionText: 'The Jackson classification of burn depth includes which categories?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Zone of hyperemia only', B: 'Zone of coagulation, zone of stasis, and zone of hyperemia', C: 'First, second, and third degree only', D: 'Partial and full thickness only' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Jackson described three zones in burn injury: coagulation (central, irreversible necrosis), stasis (surrounding area of decreased perfusion that may be salvageable), and hyperemia (outermost, increased blood flow, recovers fully).', explanationWrong: 'Hyperemia alone is incomplete. The numbered degree system and partial/full thickness are classification systems, not Jackson zones. Jackson specifically described three concentric zones of tissue injury.', topic: 'Burns and Inhalation Injury' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 56, questionText: 'The BODE index in COPD uses which four parameters?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Blood pressure, oxygen saturation, diet, and exercise', B: 'Bilirubin, output, drug levels, and electrolytes', C: 'BMI, airflow obstruction (FEV1), dyspnea (mMRC), and exercise capacity (6MWD)', D: 'Breathing rate, oxygen use, disability, and eating habits' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'BODE index: Body mass index, Obstruction (FEV1 % predicted), Dyspnea (modified MRC scale), and Exercise capacity (6-minute walk distance). It predicts mortality in COPD better than FEV1 alone.', explanationWrong: 'The other combinations are not the BODE components. BODE specifically integrates BMI, spirometry, dyspnea severity, and functional exercise capacity for prognostication.', topic: 'Acute COPD Exacerbation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 57, questionText: 'The preferred route of epinephrine administration in refractory status asthmaticus is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Oral', B: 'Intravenous or subcutaneous', C: 'Topical', D: 'Rectal' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'IV epinephrine (infusion) or subcutaneous injection provides systemic bronchodilation and hemodynamic support in near-fatal asthma when inhaled therapies are inadequate due to severe air trapping.', explanationWrong: 'Oral epinephrine has poor bioavailability. Topical and rectal routes are not used for bronchospasm. IV or subcutaneous routes ensure reliable systemic delivery in critical situations.', topic: 'Status Asthmaticus' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 58, questionText: 'The primary mechanism of death from massive pulmonary embolism is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Right ventricular failure from acute pressure overload', B: 'Left ventricular failure', C: 'Hemorrhagic shock', D: 'Sepsis' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Massive PE causes acute right ventricular pressure overload from sudden increase in pulmonary vascular resistance. The thin-walled RV cannot handle acute afterload increase, leading to RV failure, decreased LV filling, and cardiovascular collapse.', explanationWrong: 'LV failure is a consequence of RV failure (ventricular interdependence), not the primary mechanism. Hemorrhagic shock and sepsis are unrelated to PE pathophysiology.', topic: 'Pulmonary Embolism' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 59, questionText: 'Trapped lung is a condition where:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'A fibrous peel prevents the visceral pleura from expanding, causing chronic effusion', B: 'The lung has too much surfactant', C: 'There is no pleural space', D: 'The lung is over-expanded' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Trapped lung occurs when a fibrous membrane (peel) on the visceral pleura prevents lung expansion. This creates a persistent negative intrapleural pressure and chronic effusion that reforms after drainage. Decortication may be needed.', explanationWrong: 'The lung is under-expanded, not over-expanded. Surfactant is not the issue. The pleural space persists due to the inability of the lung to fill it. A fibrous peel is the defining feature.', topic: 'Pleural Diseases' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 60, questionText: 'The ICU nurse-to-patient ratio recommended for critically ill mechanically ventilated patients is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: '1:6', B: '1:4', C: '1:1 or 1:2', D: '1:8' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'Professional organizations recommend 1:1 or 1:2 nurse-to-patient ratios in the ICU for mechanically ventilated and hemodynamically unstable patients to ensure adequate monitoring, timely interventions, and patient safety.', explanationWrong: 'Ratios of 1:4-8 are appropriate for step-down or general medical units. ICU patients require intensive nursing with 1:1-2 ratios to maintain safety and care quality.', topic: 'ICU Quality Metrics' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 61, questionText: 'Volume-targeted pressure support (VTPS) modes like volume-assured pressure support (VAPS) differ from pure pressure support by:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Guaranteeing a minimum tidal volume', B: 'Providing higher FiO2', C: 'Eliminating the need for PEEP', D: 'Requiring no patient effort' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'VTPS modes guarantee a minimum tidal volume by automatically adjusting inspiratory pressure. If the patient generates insufficient VT at the set pressure support, the ventilator increases pressure to meet the volume target.', explanationWrong: 'VTPS does not affect FiO2, eliminate PEEP need, or remove patient triggering. The defining feature is the volume guarantee overlaid on a pressure support delivery mechanism.', topic: 'Advanced Mechanical Ventilation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 62, questionText: 'ScvO2 (central venous oxygen saturation) measured from a central venous catheter in the superior vena cava is typically:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Identical to SvO2', B: 'Lower than true SvO2', C: 'Higher than true SvO2 by 5-7%', D: 'Not clinically useful' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'ScvO2 is typically 5-7% higher than true SvO2 because it does not include coronary sinus blood (which has very low oxygen saturation). Normal ScvO2 is ≥ 70%, used as a surrogate for SvO2 when a PA catheter is not available.', explanationWrong: 'ScvO2 trends with SvO2 but is slightly higher, not lower or identical. It is clinically useful as a surrogate when a PA catheter is not available.', topic: 'Hemodynamic Monitoring' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 63, questionText: 'A patient with cirrhosis has pH 7.50, PaCO2 22 mmHg, HCO3- 17 mEq/L, Na+ 138, Cl- 98, albumin 2.0 g/dL. The corrected anion gap (adjusted for albumin) is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Normal', B: 'Elevated, indicating a hidden anion gap metabolic acidosis', C: 'Not calculable', D: 'Decreased' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Uncorrected AG = 138-(98+17) = 23. However, albumin correction: corrected AG = observed AG + 2.5 x (4.0 - measured albumin) = 23 + 2.5(4-2) = 28. This elevated corrected AG reveals a hidden AGMA despite apparent respiratory alkalosis.', explanationWrong: 'Without albumin correction, the AG appears mildly elevated. The corrected AG of 28 is significantly elevated, revealing an occult metabolic acidosis masked by the alkalosis and hypoalbuminemia.', topic: 'Advanced ABG Interpretation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 64, questionText: 'The concept of "baby lung" in ARDS refers to:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Ventilating neonatal patients', B: 'Using pediatric ventilator circuits', C: 'Reducing tidal volume below 4 mL/kg', D: 'The small volume of normally aerated lung tissue remaining in ARDS' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'The baby lung concept describes the small proportion of lung that remains normally aerated in ARDS (often only 200-500 mL). Tidal volumes must be scaled to this small functional volume, which is why PBW-based low VT ventilation is critical.', explanationWrong: 'The baby lung concept is not about neonates, pediatric circuits, or extreme VT reduction. It is the observation that ARDS leaves only a small normally aerated region, guiding lung-protective ventilation strategy.', topic: 'ARDS Diagnosis and Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 65, questionText: 'Dynamic compliance during mechanical ventilation is calculated using:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Plateau pressure', B: 'Mean airway pressure', C: 'Peak inspiratory pressure minus PEEP', D: 'Baseline pressure' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'Dynamic compliance = VT / (PIP - PEEP). Unlike static compliance (which uses Pplat), dynamic compliance includes the effects of airway resistance. Normal is 50-80 mL/cmH2O on a ventilator.', explanationWrong: 'Plateau pressure is used for static compliance. Mean airway pressure is used for oxygenation assessment. Baseline pressure alone is not the denominator. PIP minus PEEP is used for dynamic compliance.', topic: 'Advanced Mechanical Ventilation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 66, questionText: 'Elevated brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) in a critically ill patient with pulmonary edema suggests:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Renal failure', B: 'Hepatic failure', C: 'Pneumonia', D: 'Cardiac origin of the pulmonary edema (heart failure)' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'BNP is released by ventricular myocytes in response to volume overload and wall stress. Elevated BNP (> 500 pg/mL) in the setting of pulmonary edema strongly suggests a cardiac (cardiogenic) etiology.', explanationWrong: 'While BNP can be mildly elevated in renal failure, it is primarily a cardiac biomarker. It is not a marker of hepatic failure or pneumonia. Elevated BNP points to cardiac cause of edema.', topic: 'Hemodynamic Monitoring' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 67, questionText: 'The Stewart (physicochemical) approach to acid-base analysis uses which independent variables?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Strong ion difference (SID), total weak acids (ATOT), and PaCO2', B: 'pH and HCO3- only', C: 'Base excess only', D: 'Anion gap only' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'The Stewart approach identifies three independent variables controlling pH: strong ion difference (SID = sum of strong cations minus strong anions), total weak acid concentration (ATOT, mainly albumin and phosphate), and PaCO2.', explanationWrong: 'pH and HCO3- are dependent variables in the Stewart model. Base excess and anion gap are derived parameters. SID, ATOT, and PaCO2 are the independent determinants of pH.', topic: 'Advanced ABG Interpretation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 68, questionText: 'Conservative fluid management in ARDS (FACTT trial) compared to liberal strategy showed:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Increased mortality with conservative approach', B: 'Improved oxygenation and more ventilator-free days with conservative approach', C: 'No difference in any outcome', D: 'Increased renal failure with conservative approach' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'The FACTT trial demonstrated that conservative fluid management improved oxygenation, increased ventilator-free days, and reduced ICU LOS without increasing organ failure compared to liberal fluid strategy in ARDS.', explanationWrong: 'Conservative fluids did not increase mortality or renal failure. The trial showed clear benefits of conservative management. There were significant differences favoring the conservative approach.', topic: 'ARDS Diagnosis and Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 69, questionText: 'Corticosteroids in septic shock should be discontinued when:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Immediately after first dose', B: 'After 30 days', C: 'Never - they should continue indefinitely', D: 'When vasopressors are no longer required' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Hydrocortisone for septic shock should be tapered and discontinued when vasopressor support is no longer needed, typically after a 5-7 day course. Prolonged corticosteroid use increases infection risk and metabolic complications.', explanationWrong: 'One dose is insufficient. 30 days is too long. Indefinite use is harmful. Discontinuation should parallel vasopressor weaning, typically within 5-7 days.', topic: 'Sepsis and Septic Shock' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 70, questionText: 'Diffusion impairment as a cause of hypoxemia is characterized by:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Normal chest imaging always', B: 'Elevated PaCO2 as the primary finding', C: 'Normal A-a gradient', D: 'Hypoxemia that improves with supplemental oxygen and an elevated A-a gradient' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Diffusion impairment causes hypoxemia that responds to supplemental oxygen because increasing the alveolar PO2 gradient overcomes the diffusion limitation. It shows an elevated A-a gradient, distinguishing it from hypoventilation.', explanationWrong: 'Diffusion impairment causes an elevated (not normal) A-a gradient. PaCO2 is typically normal or low (hyperventilation). Chest imaging may show fibrosis. The hallmark is oxygen-responsive hypoxemia with elevated A-a gradient.', topic: 'Acute Respiratory Failure' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 71, questionText: 'Automated weaning protocols using closed-loop ventilator systems have been shown to:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Increase ventilator days compared to physician-driven weaning', B: 'Reduce weaning duration compared to usual care without protocols', C: 'Have no effect on outcomes', D: 'Increase mortality' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Automated/closed-loop weaning systems (SmartCare, ASV) reduce weaning duration and ventilator days compared to usual care without formal protocols, though they may not surpass well-implemented clinician-driven protocols.', explanationWrong: 'Automated weaning reduces (not increases) ventilator days. Multiple studies show benefit. There is no evidence of increased mortality. They are at least as effective as manual protocols.', topic: 'Weaning Protocols and SBTs' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 72, questionText: 'The interface used for NIV that covers both the nose and mouth is called a:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Nasal mask', B: 'Nasal pillows', C: 'Oronasal (full face) mask', D: 'Tracheostomy mask' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'The oronasal (full face) mask covers both nose and mouth and is the most commonly used interface for acute NIV in the ICU. It prevents air leak through the mouth, which is common with nasal-only masks.', explanationWrong: 'Nasal mask covers only the nose. Nasal pillows seal at the nares. Tracheostomy mask is for tracheostomy patients. The oronasal mask covers nose and mouth for acute NIV.', topic: 'NIV in Critical Care' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 73, questionText: 'The most important nursing intervention during prone positioning is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Administering prophylactic antibiotics', B: 'Frequent pressure point assessment and repositioning of the head and arms', C: 'Maintaining adequate enteral nutrition schedule', D: 'Keeping the room temperature high' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Frequent pressure point assessment and repositioning is the most critical nursing intervention during proning to prevent pressure injuries to the face, chest, and extremities. Turning the head side to side every 2 hours is essential.', explanationWrong: 'Prophylactic antibiotics and room temperature are not specific to proning. While enteral nutrition is important, pressure injury prevention through frequent assessment and repositioning is the most critical nursing concern.', topic: 'Prone Positioning' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 74, questionText: 'Which laboratory test should be monitored when a patient is receiving cisatracurium infusion?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Train-of-four peripheral nerve stimulation', B: 'Serum creatinine', C: 'Liver function tests', D: 'Thyroid function tests' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'TOF monitoring is essential during continuous cisatracurium infusion to titrate the dose and prevent excessive paralysis. The target is 1-2 twitches out of 4. BIS monitoring is also recommended to assess sedation adequacy.', explanationWrong: 'Cisatracurium undergoes Hofmann elimination (organ-independent), so renal and hepatic monitoring is less critical for drug clearance. Thyroid tests are unrelated. TOF is the specific monitoring tool.', topic: 'Neuromuscular Blocking Agents' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 75, questionText: 'The ABCDEF bundle component "F" stands for:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Family engagement and empowerment', B: 'Feeding protocol', C: 'Fever control', D: 'Fluid management' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'F = Family engagement and empowerment. The ABCDEF bundle: Assess/prevent/manage pain, Both SAT and SBT, Choice of analgesia/sedation, Delirium assessment, Early mobility, and Family engagement.', explanationWrong: 'Fluid management, feeding, and fever control are important ICU concerns but are not the F in the ABCDEF bundle. Family engagement recognizes the role of families in ICU care and recovery.', topic: 'Sedation and Pain Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 76, questionText: 'Ventilator-associated condition (VAC) is defined as a sustained increase in daily minimum FiO2 ≥ 0.20 or PEEP ≥ 3 cmH2O for at least:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: '1 hour', B: '12 hours', C: '2 calendar days', D: '1 week' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'A VAC is identified when the minimum daily FiO2 increases by ≥ 0.20 or minimum daily PEEP increases by ≥ 3 cmH2O for at least 2 calendar days after ≥ 2 days of stable or decreasing settings.', explanationWrong: '1 hour and 12 hours are too brief for the VAC definition. 1 week is too long. The CDC definition specifically requires sustained worsening for 2 calendar days.', topic: 'VAP Prevention' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 77, questionText: 'The primary difference between VV ECMO and VA ECMO is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'VV ECMO provides respiratory support only while VA ECMO provides both cardiac and respiratory support', B: 'VA ECMO provides only respiratory support', C: 'VV ECMO provides both cardiac and respiratory support', D: 'They are identical in function' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'VV ECMO provides respiratory support only by oxygenating and decarboxylating venous blood, relying on the patient native cardiac function. VA ECMO provides both cardiac and respiratory support by returning oxygenated blood to the arterial system.', explanationWrong: 'VV ECMO provides respiratory support only. VA ECMO provides both. They are fundamentally different in hemodynamic support capability. VV ECMO depends on patient cardiac function.', topic: 'ECMO' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 78, questionText: 'Rib fixation surgery (ORIF) for flail chest is associated with:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Increased pain and longer ICU stay', B: 'No change in outcomes', C: 'Reduced ventilator days, improved pulmonary function, and decreased pain', D: 'Higher infection rates with no benefit' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'Surgical stabilization of rib fractures with titanium plates has shown reduced ventilator days, shorter ICU stay, improved FVC, lower pain scores, and decreased rates of pneumonia compared to non-operative management.', explanationWrong: 'Surgical fixation improves outcomes rather than worsening pain or increasing ICU stay. Multiple studies demonstrate clear benefits. Infection rates are not increased with modern techniques.', topic: 'Chest Trauma' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 79, questionText: 'Silver sulfadiazine cream is commonly applied to burn wounds to:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Increase wound moisture only', B: 'Prevent bacterial colonization and infection', C: 'Provide cosmetic coverage', D: 'Accelerate tissue granulation' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Silver sulfadiazine is a topical antimicrobial applied to burn wounds to prevent bacterial colonization and infection. It has broad-spectrum activity against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria and some fungi.', explanationWrong: 'Silver sulfadiazine is primarily an antimicrobial, not a cosmetic or moisturizer. While maintaining a moist wound environment is a secondary benefit, infection prevention is the primary purpose.', topic: 'Burns and Inhalation Injury' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 80, questionText: 'Pulmonary rehabilitation in COPD improves:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'FEV1 significantly', B: 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin levels', C: 'Chest X-ray findings', D: 'Exercise tolerance, quality of life, and dyspnea' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Pulmonary rehabilitation improves exercise tolerance, quality of life, and dyspnea without significantly changing FEV1 or other spirometric values. Benefits come from improved muscle conditioning, breathing techniques, and self-management education.', explanationWrong: 'Pulmonary rehab does not significantly improve FEV1, CXR, or AAT levels. Its benefits are functional: improved exercise capacity (6MWD), reduced dyspnea, and better quality of life.', topic: 'Acute COPD Exacerbation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 81, questionText: 'Volumetric capnography provides which additional information compared to time-based capnography?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Heart rate', B: 'Blood pressure', C: 'Dead space fraction and CO2 elimination per breath', D: 'Hemoglobin concentration' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'Volumetric capnography plots CO2 concentration against exhaled volume (rather than time), enabling calculation of dead space fraction (VD/VT), CO2 elimination per breath, and phase III slope analysis for V/Q assessment.', explanationWrong: 'Capnography does not measure heart rate, blood pressure, or hemoglobin. Volumetric capnography unique feature is volumetric dead space and CO2 elimination quantification.', topic: 'Advanced Mechanical Ventilation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 82, questionText: 'In the management of cardiogenic shock, an intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) works by:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Continuously pumping blood like an artificial heart', B: 'Filtering blood clots', C: 'Replacing the aortic valve', D: 'Inflating during diastole and deflating during systole to augment coronary perfusion and reduce afterload' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'The IABP works by counterpulsation: inflating during diastole to augment coronary and systemic perfusion, and deflating just before systole to reduce afterload and myocardial oxygen demand.', explanationWrong: 'IABP is not a continuous pump, valve replacement, or filter. Its mechanism is counterpulsation - balloon inflation in diastole and deflation in systole to optimize cardiac hemodynamics.', topic: 'Hemodynamic Monitoring' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 83, questionText: 'Triple acid-base disorders can occur when a patient has:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Three simultaneous independent acid-base disturbances (e.g., respiratory alkalosis + AGMA + non-AGMA)', B: 'A perfectly compensated single disorder', C: 'Only one acid-base disturbance', D: 'Normal ABG values' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Triple acid-base disorders involve three simultaneous independent disturbances. For example, a septic patient may have respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation), AGMA (lactic acidosis), and metabolic alkalosis (from vomiting or NG suction).', explanationWrong: 'A single disturbance or normal values cannot be a triple disorder. Perfect compensation involves two processes, not three. Three independent simultaneous disorders define a triple acid-base disturbance.', topic: 'Advanced ABG Interpretation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 84, questionText: 'Lung compliance is decreased in ARDS because:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'The airways are dilated', B: 'Alveolar flooding, collapse, and inflammation reduce the volume of aerated lung available for ventilation', C: 'The chest wall is more compliant', D: 'Surfactant production is increased' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'ARDS reduces compliance through alveolar flooding with protein-rich edema, alveolar collapse, surfactant dysfunction, and inflammation. The remaining "baby lung" has reduced volume and compliance.', explanationWrong: 'Airway dilation would increase compliance. Increased chest wall compliance does not decrease lung compliance. Surfactant is decreased/dysfunctional in ARDS, not increased.', topic: 'ARDS Diagnosis and Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 85, questionText: 'Sepsis-associated organ dysfunction is identified by an increase in SOFA score of:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: '≥ 1 point', B: '≥ 10 points', C: '≥ 5 points', D: '≥ 2 points from baseline' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Sepsis is defined by Sepsis-3 criteria as life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection, identified by a SOFA score increase of ≥ 2 points from baseline.', explanationWrong: '≥ 1 point may not indicate significant organ dysfunction. ≥ 5 and ≥ 10 are too high. The Sepsis-3 definition specifically uses ≥ 2 points as the threshold for organ dysfunction.', topic: 'Sepsis and Septic Shock' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 86, questionText: 'Refractory hypoxemia is defined as:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'SpO2 of 95% on room air', B: 'Mild hypoxemia during sleep', C: 'PaO2 responding normally to supplemental oxygen', D: 'Hypoxemia that does not improve adequately despite maximal supplemental oxygen therapy' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Refractory hypoxemia is hypoxemia that persists despite high FiO2 (typically ≥ 0.80-1.0), indicating true shunt physiology where blood bypasses ventilated alveoli. This is characteristic of severe ARDS and may warrant ECMO consideration.', explanationWrong: 'Responding to oxygen is the opposite of refractory. Sleep-related and mild hypoxemia are not refractory. Refractory specifically means unresponsive to high-concentration oxygen therapy.', topic: 'Acute Respiratory Failure' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 87, questionText: 'The spontaneous awakening trial (SAT) should be paired with the spontaneous breathing trial (SBT) in what sequence?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'SBT first, then SAT', B: 'SAT first, then SBT if the patient passes the SAT', C: 'They should be performed on alternate days', D: 'They should never be combined' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'The "wake up and breathe" protocol (Girard ABC trial) demonstrated that performing SAT first, then SBT if the patient passes, significantly improves outcomes compared to either intervention alone.', explanationWrong: 'SBT without awakening may fail due to over-sedation. Alternating days delays liberation. Combining them is strongly recommended. The sequence is SAT first, then SBT.', topic: 'Weaning Protocols and SBTs' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 88, questionText: 'The primary physiologic effect of PEEP in mechanical ventilation is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Reducing tidal volume delivery', B: 'Increasing airway resistance', C: 'Preventing alveolar collapse at end-expiration and improving FRC', D: 'Decreasing inspiratory flow rate' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'PEEP maintains positive pressure at end-expiration, preventing alveolar collapse (atelectasis), increasing functional residual capacity (FRC), improving V/Q matching, and reducing intrapulmonary shunt.', explanationWrong: 'PEEP does not reduce VT, increase resistance, or decrease flow rate. Its primary purpose is maintaining alveolar patency and FRC at end-expiration.', topic: 'NIV in Critical Care' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 89, questionText: 'Pressure injuries during prone positioning are most common on the:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Sacrum and heels', B: 'Face (forehead, chin, cheeks), chest, and anterior iliac crests', C: 'Posterior scalp', D: 'Buttocks' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'In prone position, pressure points shift to anterior surfaces: forehead, cheeks, chin, chest, anterior iliac crests, and knees. Proper padding and frequent repositioning are essential to prevent pressure injuries in these areas.', explanationWrong: 'Sacrum, heels, posterior scalp, and buttocks are supine pressure points. Prone positioning creates pressure on anterior body surfaces and the face.', topic: 'Prone Positioning' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 90, questionText: 'The primary mechanism by which corticosteroids improve outcomes in acute COPD exacerbation is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Reduction of airway inflammation', B: 'Direct bronchodilation', C: 'Increased mucociliary clearance', D: 'Improved gas diffusion' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Corticosteroids reduce airway inflammation by suppressing inflammatory mediators, reducing mucosal edema, and decreasing mucus production. This leads to improved airflow, faster recovery, and reduced relapse rates.', explanationWrong: 'Steroids are not direct bronchodilators (that is beta-agonists). While mucociliary clearance may improve secondarily, the primary mechanism is anti-inflammatory. Gas diffusion is not directly improved.', topic: 'Acute COPD Exacerbation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 91, questionText: 'Pulsus paradoxus > 10 mmHg during severe asthma suggests:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Severe airway obstruction with large intrathoracic pressure swings', B: 'Normal respiratory mechanics', C: 'Aortic stenosis', D: 'Mild disease' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Pulsus paradoxus (decrease in SBP > 10 mmHg during inspiration) in severe asthma reflects large negative intrathoracic pressure swings from airway obstruction. Values > 20-25 mmHg indicate critical severity.', explanationWrong: 'Pulsus paradoxus indicates severe, not mild, disease. It is not normal. Aortic stenosis is a different etiology. In asthma, it directly reflects the degree of airway obstruction.', topic: 'Status Asthmaticus' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 92, questionText: 'The recommended initial dosing of alteplase (tPA) for massive PE is:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: '500 mg IV over 24 hours', B: '10 mg IV bolus', C: '100 mg IV over 2 hours', D: '50 mg oral' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'The standard dose of alteplase for massive PE is 100 mg IV over 2 hours, or as a 10 mg bolus followed by 90 mg over 2 hours. In cardiac arrest from PE, the full dose may be given as a bolus.', explanationWrong: '10 mg is too low for PE thrombolysis. 500 mg is excessive. Oral administration is not used for acute thrombolysis. The standard dose is 100 mg IV over 2 hours.', topic: 'Pulmonary Embolism' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 93, questionText: 'Indwelling pleural catheter (IPC) is indicated for:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Recurrent malignant pleural effusion in patients with trapped lung or failed pleurodesis', B: 'Simple pneumothorax', C: 'All pleural effusions', D: 'Hemothorax management' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'IPCs provide long-term outpatient drainage for recurrent symptomatic malignant pleural effusions, especially when pleurodesis has failed or cannot be performed due to trapped lung or patient preference.', explanationWrong: 'Simple pneumothorax uses chest tube or observation. Not all effusions need IPCs. Hemothorax requires large-bore chest tube. IPCs are specifically for refractory malignant effusions.', topic: 'Pleural Diseases' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 94, questionText: 'Rapid response team (RRT) activation criteria typically include:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Routine vital sign check results', B: 'Patient request for a meal', C: 'Acute change in respiratory rate, heart rate, blood pressure, or mental status', D: 'Scheduled medication administration' }), correctChoice: 'C', explanationCorrect: 'RRT activation criteria include acute changes in vital signs (RR < 8 or > 28, HR < 40 or > 130, SBP < 90, SpO2 < 90%), acute mental status change, or staff concern about patient deterioration.', explanationWrong: 'Routine vitals, meal requests, and scheduled medications are normal activities. RRT activation is for acute, concerning changes in patient condition that may indicate clinical deterioration.', topic: 'ICU Quality Metrics' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 95, questionText: 'End-inspiratory hold (plateau pressure measurement) can be performed reliably only during which ventilator mode?', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Pressure support ventilation', B: 'Volume-controlled ventilation', C: 'CPAP', D: 'Bilevel positive airway pressure' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Plateau pressure is reliably measured during volume-controlled ventilation by performing an end-inspiratory pause. In pressure-control modes, the set pressure approximates plateau pressure. PSV and CPAP do not allow reliable measurement.', explanationWrong: 'PSV is patient-triggered and cycled, making pause unreliable. CPAP has no inspiratory phase to hold. BiPAP similarly does not allow reliable pause. Volume control provides the controlled conditions needed.', topic: 'Advanced Mechanical Ventilation' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 96, questionText: 'Fluid responsiveness in a spontaneously breathing patient is best assessed by:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Static CVP value alone', B: 'Stroke volume variation (SVV)', C: 'Blood pressure cuff reading', D: 'Passive leg raise test with cardiac output monitoring' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'Passive leg raise with cardiac output monitoring is the best method for spontaneously breathing patients. SVV and PPV are unreliable during spontaneous breathing. PLR simulates a fluid challenge without actually administering fluid.', explanationWrong: 'Static CVP poorly predicts fluid responsiveness. SVV is unreliable with spontaneous breathing. BP cuff alone is insufficient. PLR with CO monitoring is the validated method for spontaneous breathers.', topic: 'Hemodynamic Monitoring' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 97, questionText: 'The ROSE trial studying neuromuscular blockade in moderate-severe ARDS found:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Strong mortality benefit with routine paralysis', B: 'Increased VAP with paralysis', C: 'Paralysis eliminated the need for sedation', D: 'No significant difference in 90-day mortality between cisatracurium and control groups using lighter sedation targets' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'The ROSE trial (2019) found no significant mortality difference between early cisatracurium and a lighter sedation control strategy in moderate-severe ARDS, leading to a more selective approach to NMB use.', explanationWrong: 'ROSE did not confirm the ACURASYS mortality benefit. VAP rates were not significantly different. Paralysis always requires adequate sedation. The key finding was no mortality difference.', topic: 'Neuromuscular Blocking Agents' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 98, questionText: 'The GCS (Glasgow Coma Scale) component that assesses verbal response in an intubated patient is scored as:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Estimated by facial expressions', B: 'Always 5 (oriented)', C: 'Omitted entirely from the GCS', D: 'Not testable and recorded as "1T" or "NT"' }), correctChoice: 'D', explanationCorrect: 'In intubated patients, the verbal component cannot be assessed and is recorded as not testable, designated "1T" or "VT" or "NT" to indicate the tube prevents assessment. The overall GCS is reported with this caveat.', explanationWrong: 'Verbal response cannot be accurately assessed or assumed normal in intubated patients. It is not simply omitted but recorded as not testable. This is a well-known limitation of GCS in intubated ICU patients.', topic: 'Sedation and Pain Management' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 99, questionText: 'Evidence-based weaning protocol implementation has been shown to:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'Increase healthcare costs without benefit', B: 'Reduce duration of mechanical ventilation by 25-50% compared to physician-directed weaning without protocols', C: 'Have no effect on weaning outcomes', D: 'Increase the rate of reintubation' }), correctChoice: 'B', explanationCorrect: 'Multiple studies demonstrate that protocolized weaning (nurse/RT-driven daily screening and SBTs) reduces ventilation duration by 25-50% compared to unstructured physician-directed weaning, without increasing reintubation rates.', explanationWrong: 'Protocols reduce costs through shorter ventilation. They clearly improve outcomes. Reintubation rates are not increased. The evidence strongly supports protocolized weaning approaches.', topic: 'Weaning Protocols and SBTs' },
      { fullExamId: exam3.id, questionIndex: 100, questionText: 'Failure-to-rescue rate in the ICU measures:', choices: JSON.stringify({ A: 'The percentage of patients who die after developing a treatable complication', B: 'The number of unsuccessful resuscitation attempts', C: 'Ventilator malfunction rates', D: 'Medication error frequency' }), correctChoice: 'A', explanationCorrect: 'Failure-to-rescue measures the proportion of patients who die after developing a major complication that could have been treated. It reflects the quality of complication recognition and management, not just complication occurrence.', explanationWrong: 'Resuscitation outcomes, ventilator malfunctions, and medication errors are separate metrics. Failure-to-rescue specifically addresses mortality from treatable complications, reflecting care quality.', topic: 'ICU Quality Metrics' }
    ],
  })

